Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have rapidly emerged as advanced crystalline porous materials in the last two decades. Owing to their ultrahigh surface areas, tunable porosities and adjustable surface group functionalities, MOFs exhibit high adsorption capacity towards various gases as well as high selectivity towards many gas mixtures.[1] Therefore, MOFs show great potential as selective adsorbents, particularly in some difficult separation systems, such as $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{4}/\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{6}$ and isomers of xylene.[2] Among these systems, $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ separation is one of the most challenging endeavors in gas separation which is of particular importance in natural gas utilization.[3] The development of efficient selective adsorbents can solve the highly energy-consuming problems caused by the current method of cryogenic distillation. However, most of the traditional porous materials including zeolites do not show sufficient separation towards $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ for industrial application because of the very similar properties of methane and nitrogen molecules in terms of diameter, polarity, etc. Furthermore, previous studies have shown that zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a particular interesting class of MOFs, exhibited preferential $C H_{4}$ adsorption over $N_{2}$ .[4] Theoretical studies revealed that the preferential adsorption sites for $C H_{4}$ on
ZIF-8 are located in specific regions close to the organic imidazolate linker, suggesting that the imidazolate linkers facilitate the adsorption of methane.[5] Inspired by the reported theoretical results, herein, we report a strategy by introducing ZIF-8 subunits, ZIF-14 subunits and their derivatives into traditional zeolites to increase the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity of traditional zeolites. The low cost and mature synthesis technology of zeolites make it possible for this strategy to overcome the problems faced by MOFs, such as complicated synthesis processes, high costs and forming difficulties, and to realize the combination of advanced MOFs and traditional zeolites. ZIF-8 subunits (Zn-mIM, mIM $^{=2}$ -methylimidazole), ZIF-14 subunits (Zn-eIM, eIM $\scriptstyle1=2$ -ethylimidazole) and their derivatives Zn-pIM (pIM=2-propylimidazole) were used to decorate zeolite Y and zeolite ZSM-5.
The interaction energies (IE) between the adsorbent and adsorbate molecules were obtained by density functional theory (DFT) calculations (see the Supporting Information). The $\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ cluster was adopted as the zeolite framework model.[6] The optimized conformational geometries and interaction energies between adsorbate molecule of methane or nitrogen and adsorbent clusters of $Z\mathsf{n}^{2+}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ , Zn-mIM/Si(1)Al(1), Zn$\mathsf{e l M}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ and $Z n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{p l M}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ was schematically illustrated in Figure 1. It was indicated that the IE between $C H_{4}$ and decorated zeolite clusters $(Z\mathsf{n}{\mathsf{-m l M}}/{\mathsf{S i}}(1){\mathsf{A l}}(1)$ , Zn$\mathsf{e l M}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ and $Z n\mathrm{-p}|\mathsf{M}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A}|(1)\bigr)$ were much lower than those of the $Z\mathsf{n}^{2+}/\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ cluster, while the IE between ${\sf N}_{2}$ and decorated zeolite clusters increased obviously, theoretically suggesting that with the decoration of the ZIF subunit, the $\mathsf{S i}(1)\mathsf{A l}(1)$ cluster has stronger electrostatic interactions with $C H_{4}$ and weaker electrostatic interactions with ${\sf N}_{2}$ . In other words, the incorporation of Zn-mIM, Zn-eIM and Zn-pIM may result in higher $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity of zeolite.
First, the pristine zeolites (NaY or HZSM-5) were ionexchanged to zinc form. Second, the as-prepared zinc form zeolites were reacted with the imidazoles (see the Supporting Information). A typical adsorbent prepared with NaY, ionexchanged to $Z n\mathsf{Y}$ form and then reacted with 2-methylimidazole, was denoted as $Z n Y-m l M$ (for 2-ethylimidazole and 2- propylimidazole, it would be $Z n Y-e l M$ and ZnY-pIM respectively). The proposed process and typical structures of the adsorbents are illustrated in Figure 2. For the first step, $^{23}{\mathsf{N a}}$ MAS NMR spectra indicated that $Z n^{2+}$ were exchanged with $\mathsf{N a^{+}}$ inside zeolite framework, confirming the existence of $Z\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ inside zeolite framework (Figure S2).[7] Zn 2p XPS spectra (Figure S3) exhibited the two characteristic peaks of $Z n$ corresponding to Zn $2p_{1/2}$ and Zn $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ , respectively, were observed of the resulting materials.[8] $^{23}\mathsf{N a}$ MAS NMR, Zn 2p XPS and ICP-OES (Table S1) results confirmed the existence of $Z n^{2+}$ inside zeolite framework and the successful preparation of the zinc form zeolite. For the second step, it is the covalent binding of imidazoles and $Z n^{2+}$ inside zeolite pores. In the FTIR spectra
(Figure S4), adsorption bands at $3138\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , $2979\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , 2935 cm1, $1473\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1432\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ could be attributed to $C-H$ stretching and deformation vibrations of imidazole.[9] Additional bands at $1569\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ can be assigned to $C=N$ stretching vibration.[10] It can be seen that the imidazole characteristic IR bands of $Z n Y-m l M$ , ZnY-eIM and $Z n Y-p|M$ matches well with that of ZIF-8. The absorption band at 420 cm−1 was observed for the Zn-N stretching vibration,[10a, 11] indicating the covalent of imidazole and $Z n^{2+}$ in the zeolite. The FTIR spectra results confirms the functionalization of ZIF subunits into zeolite. $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP/MAS NMR results of decorated samples also shown resonances which are the typical resonances of mIM, eIM and pIM, respectively (Figure S5).[12] The assignment of 15N CP/MAS NMR signals has been made for pure imidazoles (mIM, eIM and pIM) and ZIF-8 in Figure 3 and Figure S6.[13] For pure imidazoles, two different N signals were observed. While for ZIF-8, both of the N in imidazole were linked with Zn, only a single resonance at 204.5 ppm appeared. Compared with pure imidazoles and ZIF-8, it was very clear that among the decorated adsorbents, 2 signals appeared, for which one of the resonance agreed well with the N of imidazoles in lower chemical shift, while the other was in accordance with the N of ZIF-8. Thus, for the decorated adsorbents, one of the N of imidazole bonded to $Z n^{2+}$ and the other N remain the same with pure imidazoles, implying the formation of ZIF subunits of Zn-IM. $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP/MAS NMR spectra and $^{15}{\mathsf{N}}$ CP/MAS NMR spectra further confirmed that zinc cations were existed inside the zeolite framework and the formation of ZIF subunit inside zeolite pores. Zn 2p XPS spectra demonstrated that the binding energy of decorated samples shifted toward lower values compared to pristine $Z n\Upsilon$ and close to ZIF-8, implying the information of ZIFs fragment (Figure S3). In HP- $^{129}\mathsf{X e}$ NMR spectra (Figure S7), higher chemical shift of xeon were observed in ZnY-pIM and ZnZSM-5-pIM compared with $Z n\Upsilon$ and ZnZSM-5, indicated that xenon is adsorbed in a highly confined space, in other words, the pore size of the zeolites become smaller after the decoration.[14] Therefore, the entirety of the zeolite crystals are functionalized and not just the pore mouth. TEM images also indirectly suggested that the pore mouth also be functionalized (Figure S8). The TGA results indicated that the combination of ZIF subunits and zeolites were highly thermally stable (Figure.S9). The successful functionalization of ZIF subunits into zeolites can also be confirmed by elemental analysis and BET results (Table S1). PXRD patterns indicated that major diffraction peaks of the decorated zeolites agreed well with the pristine zeolites, confirming that these zeolites remained intact after modification of the ZIF subunit (Figure S10-S11). SEM images revealed no significant morphological changes in the zeolites after modification (Figure S12-S13). ${\sf N}_{2}$ $Z n\mathsf{Y}$
Fig 4 shows the $C H_{4}$ and adsorption isotherms of , $Z n Y-m l M$ , ZnY-eIM, ZnY-pIM, ZnZSM-5, ZnZSM-5-mIM, ZnZSM-5-eIM and ZnZSM-5-pIM. $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption selectivities for equimolar $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ mixtures at $100~\mathsf{k P a}$ and $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ of samples were calculated by ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) after isotherms were fit to the dual-site Langmuir-Freundlich (DLSF) equation (Supporting Information).[15] Fitting parameters and correlation coefficients of the DSLF isotherms are presented in Table S3. Compared with NaY (Figure S14), $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of $Z n\Upsilon$ (Fig 4a and 4b) both decreased and $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity was almost unchanged. When modified with the mIM linker, the decrease in ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake was more than that in $C H_{4}$ uptake; thus, the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity increased from 1.6 of pristine NaY and 2.1 of $Z n\mathsf{Y}$ increased to 4.25. After modification of the eIM linker, the $C H_{4}$ uptake of the adsorbent $(0.32\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , $100\mathsf{k P a}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) was much higher than that of pristine $Z n\Upsilon$ $(0.25\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ , $100~\mathsf{k P a},$ , $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ ) while ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake decreased, which agreed well with the molecular simulation predictions. A higher $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 4.6 was achieved. When modified with the pIM linker, the bigger propyl group resulted in obvious decrease in $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptakes. The decrease in ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake was more than that in $C H_{4}$ uptake and a high $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 7.56 was achieved. Furthermore, the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity was higher than that of ZIF-8 (4.23) at $100\mathsf{k P a}$ and $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S15). It is reported that ZIF14 cannot serve as a potential gas-sorption material due to its
small aperture $(2.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ and $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{B E T}}$ $(28.7~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g})$ .[16] Thus, ZIF-14 did not use as a reference in this work, while its subunit was used to decorate zeolites. Such an interesting phenomenon suggests that the strategy of decorating zeolite with the ZIF subunits to improve $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity achieved desirable results. The zeolite ZSM-5, $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of zinc form varied slightly from HZSM-5 (Figure S16) as well as $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity. After modification of the mIM, eIM and pIM linkers, the $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake decreased; nevertheless, the amount of ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorbed was reduced dramatically from $0.22\mathrm{mmol}/9$ of ZnZSM-5 to 0.09 mmol/g of ZnZSM-5-mIM, 0.07 mmol/g ZnZSM-5-eIM and 0.05 mmol/g ZnZSM-5-pIM at $100~\mathsf{k P a}$ and $25\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Therefore, the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivities obviously increased from 4.25 for ZnZSM-5 to 5.94 for ZnZSM-5-mIM, 6.23 for ZnZSM-5-eIM and 8.44 for ZnZSM-5-pIM at $100\mathsf{k P a}$ and $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . $C H_{4/}N_{2}$ selectivities of the resulted samples are also quite comparable to the current top performing and typical materials (Table S4). ${\mathsf{H P}}{\mathsf{-}}^{129}{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{e}}$ NMR spectra (Figure S7) showed that compared with pristine zinc form zeolites, the broadening linewidth of the decorated zeolites demonstrated the decrease of the mobility of xenon which means the diffusion of the xenon was affected.[17] Adsorption kinetic curves exhibited that the $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of the samples were all fast and soon reach to equilibrium (Figure S17). Diffusion time constants $(\mathsf{D}/\mathsf{r}^{2})$ of $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ also indicated that the diffusion of $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ were slightly effected after the decoration (Table S5). The resulted samples have good reproducibility and thermal stability as well (Figure S18-S25). All the results indicated that the incorporation of ZIF subunits into traditional zeolites can greatly improve the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity as expected from the molecular simulation results.
To evaluate the dynamic separation performances of the adsorbents, breakthrough experiments were carried out with $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}/\mathsf{H e}$ (10/10/80) mixtures. Breakthrough curves of $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ mixtures on two typical samples of ZnY-pIM and ZnZSM-5-pIM are presented in Figure 5. For both of the adsorbents, ${\sf N}_{2}$ firstly eluted from the packed bed whereas $C H_{4}$ was retained and then reached saturation. ZnY-pIM and ZnZSM-5-pIM exhibited efficient dynamic separation performances. Compared with pristine $Z n\mathsf{Y}$ , the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ dynamic separation performance was greatly enhanced (Figure S26). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ breakthrough of ZnY-eIM was slightly earlier than that of $Z n\mathsf{Y}$ , while the breakthrough of $C H_{4}$ was much later. $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ separation performance of NaY was also poorer than that of ZnY-eIM (Figure S27). The cycle and regeneration capabilities of ZnY-pIM and ZnZSM-5-pIM were further studied by breakthrough cycle experiments (Figure S28).
In summary, we report a series of new adsorbents by introducing ZIF subunits into traditional zeolite Y and zeolite ZSM-5. Both the molecular simulation and experimental results indicate that the strategy of incorporating Zn-mIM, Zn-eIM and Zn-pIM into zeolite Y and zeolite ZSM-5 resulted in significant enhancement in the $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity of the adsorbents, which were higher than those of pristine zeolites and even ZIFs. Notably, resulting adsorbents exhibited high thermal stability up to $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $600~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ which makes the adsorbents very applicable. This strategy has combined traditional zeolites and MOFs perfectly, which can solve the complicated synthesis, high costs and forming difficulties of MOFs as well as the problem of low $\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity in traditional zeolites. This concept provides an idea for the design of new MOFs-Zeolite composite materials, ranging from various adsorbents to catalysts.
